<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Theocritus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 300–after 260 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Theokritos</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Theocritus was a Greek poet from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magna-graecia">Magna Graecia</a>, and the creator of ancient Greek pastoral poetry. Little is known of his life beyond what his poems suggest: an ancient epigram makes him a Syracusan, the son of Praxagoras and Philinna, and he probably lived for a time in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, and perhaps on the island of Kos. His Idylls, written in the Doric dialect, include bucolic poems set in the country, such as the death of the herdsman Daphnis and the Cyclops Polyphemus's love for the sea-nymph Galatea, and mimes of town life. His poems praising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiero-ii-of-syracuse">Hiero II of Syracuse</a> and Ptolemy Philadelphus are the only ones that can be dated. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgil">Virgil</a> knew his work.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/theocritus.jpg" alt="Theocritus" width="330" height="330" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Print made by: Anonymous · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_bust_of_Theocritus_in_profile_to_left,_after_an_antique_marble_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
